<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Northern War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as First Northern War, Northern War of 1655–1660 · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Northern War (1655–1660) was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> and its adversaries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">The Great Elector</a> changed sides from Sweden to Poland and won full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> by the treaties of Labiau and Wehlau in 1656–1657, confirmed at the Peace of Oliva in 1660.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-northern-war.jpg" alt="Second Northern War" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imonoz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Northern_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> as a major power and replaced Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> as the leading state in the Baltic. Sweden remained at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, though a truce had been renewed since 1630. The Commonwealth was weakened by rebellions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> and by conflict between King John II Casimir Vasa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>.</p><p>When Russian troops invaded in the Russo-Polish War (1654-67) and neared the Baltic coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> intervened, claiming to protect Polish Protestants. Talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> ended in February 1655 without result because John II Casimir would not drop his claims to the Swedish crown, and Sweden decided on a preemptive attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Swedish forces entered the Commonwealth in July 1655 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. The nobles of Greater Poland surrendered at Ujście, and by the Treaty of Kėdainiai Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a> placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> under Swedish protection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 8 September, won at Żarnów and Wojnicz, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> surrendered on 19 October. John II Casimir went into exile in Silesia. The rapid occupation became known in Poland as the deluge.</p><p>Resistance grew after Swedes looted Catholic churches and mistreated clergy. The monastery of Jasna Góra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> withstood a siege from November 1655 to January 1656, and John II Casimir returned on 1 January 1656. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in May 1656. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, allied with Sweden, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> of 17 January 1656 and later full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the Treaty of Labiau. In 1657 it changed sides, winning Polish withdrawal of claims in the treaties of Wehlau and Bromberg.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> declared war in 1657, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>. In February 1658 a Swedish army crossed the frozen Belts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> on 26 February 1658, yielding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Trøndelag. Sweden attacked Denmark again in August 1658, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> did not surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> died on 23 February 1660, and the Treaty of Oliva was signed on 23 April. Sweden was accepted as sovereign in Swedish Livonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a>, and John II Casimir withdrew his claims to the Swedish throne. Occupied territories were restored to their earlier sovereigns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-copenhagen-1660">Treaty of Copenhagen</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Trøndelag to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and the borders it set among Denmark, Sweden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> have lasted to the present day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> settled with Sweden in the Treaty of Cardis in 1661. Historians are split on whether Sweden or the Commonwealth emerged the victor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern War of 1655–1660” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He built a permanent standing army that grew to some 30,000 men, fought on both sides in the <b>Second Northern War</b> to win sovereignty over the Duchy of Prussia, and on 28 June 1675 defeated an invading Swedish army at the Battle of Fehrbellin, the first major victory…</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Hajo Holborn, this major clash of the <b>Second Northern War</b> marked the beginning of Prussian military history.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
